Hi,

I got three answers from Johan Brusche, Dr Thomas Blinn and Raul Sossa Sossa. 
Many thanks to you.

After reading the answers, there are 4 things I want to investigate:

1) have a talk with the SAN-guy considering zoning (new zoning recently?)
2) reboot the server (just to be sure no transient electrical problem)
3) upgrade the emx driver (to be sure that it isn't the driver which gives 
problem)
The newest driver is v.2.14 
(http://h30097.www3.hp.com/unix/EarlyReleasePatch-download.html), but the 
server needs the PK6 installed.
Or just a driver which > 2.02; I could not find any from HP site.
(NB: according to Dr Blinn, HP does not release
these independently for older patch levels that are no longer supported, so 
you can't find it on their web site)
4) upgrade to PK6

Best regards,
Tuan


**************

Answer from Johan Brusche:
>You probably have "other" operating systems using the same SAN, and
>you have NOT used zoning on the switches to separate them.

>Johan.

**************

Answer fr Dr Thomas Blinn:

>Hi, Tuan.
>
>I passed your question along to our emx driver expert, and he
>had these comments:
>
>> Hi Tom,
>>
>> 	The emx driver issued a request to the Name Server to get all
>> the port IDs for a particular port type. The request was rejected with
>> a reason code of "Unable to perform request" and an explanation code
>> of "No port type". I'm not sure under what context this is occurring
>> given that the error has no useful information other than the error
>> message itself. This does not feel like a driver issue but rather
>> something going on with the fabric. Is anything going on with the
>> fabric that could cause the port IDs of that the type we might be
